ns=""><enttyp><enttypl>RISIKOGEB_HQ100_l5</enttypl><enttypt>polygon</enttypt><enttypc>23776</enttypc><enttypd>Each entity represents a spatial feature (polygon).</enttypd><enttypds>Author-defined, based on analytical purpose and processing logic of this study, following ISO 19115 metadata guidance and ArcGIS Pro documentation standards.</enttypds></enttyp><attr xmlns=""><attrlabl>P_change</attrlabl><attrdef>The Population Change Index calculates the velocity of demographic expansion between 2002 and 2025. It identifies growth hotspots to differentiate between "legacy risk" (long-established populations) and "newly created risk" (modern developments in flood-prone zones).</attrdef><attrdefs>P_change = (TOT_2025 - TOT_2002)/TOT_2002*100. This metric reflects the evolution of human "value-at-risk" within static hydrological boundaries.</attrdefs><attrtype>double</attrtype><attwidth>19</attwidth><attrdomv><rdom xmlns=""><rdommin>-39.923954</rdommin><rdommax>85.276578</rdommax></rdom></attrdomv></attr><attr xmlns=""><attrlabl>Inte_Exp</attrlabl><attrdef>The Internationalization Index quantifies the proportion of foreign nationals within the exposed population to measure "Information Vulnerability". It identifies areas where traditional mono-lingual emergency protocols may fail due to language barriers or smaller local support networks.</attrdef><attrdefs>Inte_index = INT_2025/TOT_2025*100. It serves as a proxy for the recipient's ability to decode emergency broadcasts and navigate municipal evacuation protocols.</attrdefs><attrtype>double</attrtype><attwidth>19</attwidth><attrdomv><rdom xmlns=""><rdommin>1.45808</rdommin><rdommax>42.280223</rdommax></rdom></attrdomv></attr></detailed></eainfo><mdDateSt Sync="TRUE">20260122</mdDateSt><mdChar><CharSetCd value="004" Sync="TRUE"/></mdChar><mdContact xmlns=""><editorSource>external</editorSource><editorDigest>6045feea04197ffe86dafd59883a4fac69006bce</editorDigest><rpIndName>Qinwei Zhu</rpIndName><rpOrgName>PLUS-ZGIS</rpOrgNam